body:
It looks to me like the problem is not with the lambda itself, but rather the way your dictionary is organized, and may be an issue with dictionaries themselves; I played with your code example a bit, and adding a comma after `print(b)`, or wrapping the whole function in parentheses, both "fix" the error, with some caveats:
- the comma is in a very unnatural place when typing `print(b),`
- the closing parenthesis when wrapping the function is also wrong:
```gdscript
"function": (func(a: int, b: int) -> void:
	if a > b:
		print(a)
	elif b > a:
		print(b))  # putting the parenthesis after the print statement works
		)  # this parenthesis works
	)  # this parenthesis works
)  # this parenthesis produces the error, when it should be the correct indentation
```
```gdscript
"function": (
	func(a: int, b: int) -> void:
		if a > b:
			print(a)
		elif b > a:
			print(b))  # putting the parenthesis after the print statement works
		)  # this parenthesis works
	)  # this parenthesis produces the error
)  # this parenthesis produces the error, when it should be the correct indentation
```
Adding a trailing comma after the closing parenthesis does not change the behavior.

Note that I tested this on the master branch, so this is still reproducible in v4.5.beta.custom_build [c81fd6c51].

Also, to confirm that this issue isn't about lambdas: copying the function out of the dictionary, and adding `var function := func(a: int, b: int) -> void:` works without any error.
